Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 18:44:40 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 0/3] media: v4l2-jpeg: fix segment length handling in the header parser
To: Mauro Carvalho Chehab <mchehab@kernel.org>
Cc: Hans Verkuil <hverkuil@kernel.org>,
    Philipp Zabel <p.zabel@pengutronix.de>,
    Mirela Rabulea <mirela.rabulea@nxp.com>,
    imx@lists.linux.dev,
    linux-media@vger.kernel.org,
    linux-kernel@vger.kernel.org

v4l2_jpeg_parse_header() can move backwards in the buffer: the DHT
parser ends every segment with a skip of -2 bytes, which jpeg_skip()
turns into a wrapped size_t and accepts, and tables that overrun their
DHT, DQT or APP14 segment leave negative lengths that end the same way.
Malformed tables can be accepted, and some well-formed but unusual
segments (a DHT ending in ff db, trailing bytes in a DQT) fail.

Patch 1 fixes the end of DHT parsing and rejects DHT tables that overrun
their segment, patch 2 does the same for DQT and skips the rest of the
segment, and patch 3 bounds jpeg_skip() by the bytes left, so negative
lengths fail, which also fixes short APP14 segments. Patch 3 needs
patch 1 (said in its message): without it every DHT segment would fail.

How it was found and tested:

- Found while writing a JPEG decoder for the Allwinner A10/A20 video
  engine (not upstream) that uses v4l2_jpeg_parse_header(): the parser
  built for the host under ASan/UBSan reports the pointer overflow in
  jpeg_skip() on the first frame of every JPEG with a DHT segment. The
  kernel build (-fno-strict-overflow) treats it as wrapping; the bug is
  that parsing resumes two bytes back.
- Host harness (the file built with clang ASan/UBSan, each input in a
  buffer of its exact size), v7.3 vs v7.3 + each patch of the series:
  - 1180 frames from 543 files (libjpeg-turbo, ffmpeg, PIL,
    ImageMagick, a Logitech C170 camera incl. its broken frames,
    fault-injected and hand-made files; 4:2:0, 4:2:2, 4:4:4, gray,
    CMYK, with and without DHT, DRI and Adobe APP14), each parsed with
    and without table arrays: identical results after every patch
    (every offset, length and field), the same 9 frames rejected
    (progressive, sampling factors the helper does not support);
  - crafted inputs, each fixed by its own patch: BITS overrunning the
    DHT segment were accepted for 2 of 5 base files (rejected by chance
    for the others) and a DHT ending in ff db failed with -EINVAL
    (patch 1); a 16-bit table overrunning the DQT segment was accepted
    and a DQT with trailing ff c2 bytes failed (patch 2); an 8-byte
    Adobe APP14 segment was accepted (patch 3);
  - with the whole series, every truncation of the header region plus
    3000 random byte and bit mutations per frame, for 488 frames (5.85
    million parses): no sanitizer report and no hang.
- W=1 build for ARM (sunxi_defconfig + COMPILE_TEST with coda and
  imx-jpeg, the two in-tree users) after each patch, no new warnings.
  sparse and smatch were not run.
- v4l2-jpeg.c is the same in 6.18 and v7.3. The series without the
  16-bit DQT check runs in the A20 decoder on a Cubietruck since
  2026-10-25 (8-bit tables only, so the 16-bit check is host-tested
  only). Not tested on coda or imx-jpeg hardware.
- Applies unchanged to media-committers next (d7e7bca71698): its
  v4l2-jpeg.c is identical to v7.3.
- Callers: coda passes table arrays and sees the stricter DHT/DQT
  segment handling (a segment that declares more bytes than its tables
  is now skipped in full); imx-jpeg passes none and is only affected
  by the jpeg_skip() bound (short APP14 segments now fail).

No Cc: stable: the backwards skips never leave the buffer, so these are
parse errors and acceptance of malformed tables, not memory safety
issues; I leave stable to your judgement.

The patches and this letter were written with an AI assistant; I
reviewed and tested them.

D.R.racer (3):
  media: v4l2-jpeg: fix the end of DHT segment parsing
  media: v4l2-jpeg: bound DQT tables and skip the rest of the segment
  media: v4l2-jpeg: bound jpeg_skip() by the bytes left

